  "text": " tasked with finding the common ratio of the new geometric sequence after removing the seventh term from the original arithmetic sequence. Let's begin by determining the general formula for the arithmetic sequence.\n\n1. **Finding the common difference \\( d \\) and the first term \\( a_1 \\):**\n\nGiven that the first term \\( a_1 = 5 \\) and the fourth term \\( a_4 = 20 \\):\n\n- Using the formula for the \\( n \\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence, \\( a_n = a_1 + (n-1)d \\),\n\nwe can set up the equation for the fourth term:\n\n\\[\na_4 = a_1 + 3d\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\n\\[\n20 = 5 + 3d\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( d \\):\n\n\\[\n15 = 3d \\implies d = 5\n\\]\n\nThus, the formula for the \\( n \\)-th term of the arithmetic sequence is:\n\n\\[\na_n = 5 + (n-1) \\cdot 5 = 5n\n\\]\n\n2. **Generating the arithmetic sequence:**\n\nGiven the terms \\( 5, 10, 15, 20, \\ldots \\).\n\n3. **Removing the seventh term:**\n\nThe seventh term \\( a_7 \\) can be found by substituting \\( n = 7 \\):\n\n\\[\na_7 = 5 \\cdot 7 = 35\n\\]\n\nRemoving the seventh term from the sequence, we have the sequence \\( 5, 10, 15, 20, 30, 35, \\ldots \\).\n\n4. **Generating the new geometric sequence:**\n\nNotice that the remaining terms \\( 5, 10, 15, 20, 30, \\ldots \\) provide a clue. Rewriting the sequence for clarity:\n\n\\[\n5, 10, 15, 20, 30\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the common ratio \\( r \\) of this new geometric sequence. The ratio between consecutive terms is:\n\n\\[\nr = \\frac{10}{5} = \\frac{15}{10} = \\frac{20}{15} = \\frac{30}{20} = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the common ratio \\( r \\) of the new geometric sequence is:\n\n\\boxed{2}\n.",
